Changes we make to the data
---------------------------
Unless an entry says more, our changes are changes of form. We load each source into
SQLite tables and search indexes, keep a plain-text copy of HTML or USFM markup, and
serve excerpts through the chat tools. Data under CC BY-SA 4.0, and our changes to it,
remain under CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/). Data under
CC BY 4.0 is used under https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/.

Study Bible data (bundled in db/study_bible.db and db/theology.db)
------------------------------------------------------------------
Scripture texts
- STEPBible-Data, by Tyndale House, Cambridge. CC BY 4.0.
  Tagged Hebrew OT (TAHOT), tagged Greek NT (TAGNT), lexicons (TBESH, TBESG, TFLSJ),
  proper names (TIPNR), and morphology code expansions (TEHMC, TEGMC).
  Credit: "STEP Bible", https://www.STEPBible.org
  Source: https://github.com/STEPBible/STEPBible-Data
- Readable English translations, all public domain, as USFM from eBible.org
  (https://ebible.org). They power lookup_verse, get_passage, and compare_translations.
  Footnotes and cross-reference notes are removed. The verse text is unchanged.
    * Berean Standard Bible (BSB), from BSB Publishing.
    * World English Bible (WEB). "World English Bible" is a trademark of eBible.org.
      We serve the text verbatim, and modified text must not be called the WEB.
    * King James Version (KJV, 1769 standardized text). Public domain outside the UK.
      In the UK it is under Crown letters patent.
    * American Standard Version (ASV, 1901).
    * Young's Literal Translation (YLT), by Robert Young.
- Greek Septuagint. H.B. Swete, "The Old Testament in Greek according to the
  Septuagint" (Cambridge, 1909-1930). Public domain. Greek word forms only.
  Machine-readable digitization via https://github.com/eliranwong/LXX-Swete-1930
  (that repository is GPL-3.0, and the underlying Swete text is public domain).
  Powers get_septuagint.
- English Septuagint. Sir Lancelot C.L. Brenton, "The Septuagint version of the
  Old Testament, with an English translation" (1851). Public domain. Verse-by-verse
  English of the Greek LXX, in LXX versification, paired to the Greek in get_septuagint.
  Machine-readable USFM via eBible.org (eng-Brenton).
- Versification mappings. Copenhagen Alliance, versification-specification
  (standard-mappings lxx.json and eng.json). Data CC BY-SA 4.0. Maps LXX verse numbers
  to standard numbering. We load the two mapping files into one table.
  Source: https://github.com/Copenhagen-Alliance/versification-specification

Lexicons
- Brown-Driver-Briggs, "A Hebrew and English Lexicon of the Old Testament" (1906).
  Public domain. Machine-readable JSON (DictBDB.json) formatted by Eliran Wong from a
  file provided by Tim Morton (Bible Analyzer). The repository states it is a public
  domain document. Source: https://github.com/eliranwong/unabridged-BDB-Hebrew-lexicon
- Abbott-Smith, "A Manual Greek Lexicon of the New Testament". Public domain. TEI
  edition via https://github.com/translatable-exegetical-tools/Abbott-Smith (the
  repository states the lexicon and its markup are public domain).

People, places, and events
- Theographic Bible Metadata, by Robert Rouse (viz.bible). CC BY-SA 4.0.
  People, places, events, genealogy, and verse-entity mentions powering the
  graph tools. Source: https://github.com/robertrouse/theographic-bible-metadata
- ACAI Biblical Entity Data. Copyright 2025 Mission Mutual (the license file credits
  the 2024 release to BiblioNexus). CC BY-SA 4.0. People, places, groups, and key
  terms. We keep selected fields (names, descriptions, roles, family links, and up to
  20 references per entity). Source: https://github.com/BibleAquifer/ACAI

Cross-references and parallels
- OpenBible.info cross-references. CC BY 4.0.
  ~344k community-voted cross-references (TSK-derived). Powers get_cross_references.
  Source: https://www.openbible.info/labs/cross-references/
- UBS Parallel Passage Database. (c) 2023 United Bible Societies. CC BY-SA 4.0.
  Editorial parallel-passage groups, from TheologAI's vendored snapshot. Powers
  get_parallel_passages. Source: https://github.com/ubsicap/ubs-open-license

Study notes and reference
- Aquifer Open Study Notes. (c) 2026 Mission Mutual (https://missionmutual.org).
  CC BY-SA 4.0. An adaptation of Tyndale Open Study Notes, (c) 2023 Tyndale House
  Publishers, CC BY-SA 4.0. Powers get_study_notes.
  Source: https://github.com/BibleAquifer/AquiferOpenStudyNotes
- Aquifer Open Bible Dictionary. (c) 2026 Mission Mutual (https://missionmutual.org).
  CC BY-SA 4.0. An adaptation of Tyndale Open Bible Dictionary, (c) 2023 Tyndale House
  Publishers, CC BY-SA 4.0. Powers get_bible_dictionary.
  Source: https://github.com/BibleAquifer/AquiferOpenBibleDictionary
- unfoldingWord(R) Translation Notes. (c) 2022 unfoldingWord (https://unfoldingword.org).
  CC BY-SA 4.0. Powers get_study_notes.
  Source: https://github.com/BibleAquifer/UWTranslationNotes
- Open Translator's Notes (SIL). (c) 2024 SIL International (https://opentn.bible).
  CC BY-SA 4.0. Powers get_study_notes.
  Source: https://github.com/BibleAquifer/SILOpenTranslatorsNotes
- Familiarization, Internalization, Articulation (FIA) Key Terms. (c) 2025 Word
  Collective (https://wordcollective.com). CC BY-SA 4.0. Powers get_key_terms.
  Source: https://github.com/BibleAquifer/FIAKeyTerms
- Easton's Bible Dictionary. M.G. Easton, "Illustrated Bible Dictionary" (1897).
  Public domain. ~3,960 headword articles powering get_bible_dictionary, alongside the
  Aquifer Open Bible Dictionary. Machine-readable JSON via
  https://github.com/garydavenport73/eastons-bible-dictionary-json (an MIT-licensed
  repository, and the underlying text is public domain).
- Spurgeon's "Morning and Evening". C.H. Spurgeon (1865). Public domain.
  366 verse-keyed daily devotional readings (morning and evening). Powers
  get_devotional. Machine-readable JSON via
  https://github.com/dwahrens/morning-and-evening (an Apache-2.0 wrapper, and the
  underlying text is public domain).
- Word-frequency index. Derived data, computed at build time from the bundled
  STEPBible tagged text (no external source). Corpus occurrence counts per Strong's
  number. Powers reader-mode vocabulary (vocabulary_list, reader_mode).

Commentaries and the historic church
- Public-domain commentaries via the HelloAO Free Use Bible API (each marked public
  domain). They power get_commentary: Matthew Henry's Commentary, John Gill's
  Exposition, Jamieson-Fausset-Brown, Adam Clarke's Commentary, Calvin's
  Commentaries, and Keil & Delitzsch (Old Testament). Source: https://bible.helloao.org
- C.H. Spurgeon, "The Treasury of David". Public domain. Spurgeon's own exposition of
  the Psalms only, verse-keyed for get_commentary. Markdown edition via
  https://github.com/lyteword/chspurgeon-tod (CC0 1.0).
- Charles Hodge, "A Commentary on the Epistle to the Romans". Public domain (Hodge
  died in 1878). Passage-keyed for get_commentary from an EPUB of the public-domain text.
- J.C. Ryle, "Expository Thoughts on the Gospels". Public domain (Ryle died in 1900).
  Passage-keyed for get_commentary from an EPUB of the public-domain text.
- Patristic catena, from the HistoricalChristianFaith Commentaries-Database (released
  to the public domain by its compilers). ~85,600
  public-domain church-father comment excerpts (authors before 1900 only, and later
  fair-use excerpts are excluded at import). Powers get_church_fathers.
  Source: https://github.com/HistoricalChristianFaith/Commentaries-Database
- Patristic quotations. SermonIndex, "Early Church Fathers: Scripture Citation Index"
  (2026). CC BY 4.0 for the verse alignment and name normalization. The patristic
  translations themselves are public domain. We add only the excerpts the
  HistoricalChristianFaith catena lacks, and fill in each author's date and category
  from that catena. Powers get_church_fathers.
  Source: https://huggingface.co/datasets/sermonindex/early-church-fathers
- Historical documents (creeds, confessions, catechisms). Public-domain texts,
  compiled by TJ-Frederick/TheologAI (ISC-licensed compilation). 17 documents
  across the Ecumenical, Reformed, Lutheran, Anglican, Catholic, and Orthodox
  traditions. They power get_confession. Source: https://github.com/TJ-Frederick/TheologAI
- Reformed Standards Compendium (confessional.tech / reformed-standards). Apache
  License 2.0. Confession and catechism texts with structured Scripture proof-texts
  (OSIS). Powers the verse-to-confession graph (find_confessions_by_verse,
  get_confession_proofs) and adds net-new standards (Savoy, Westminster PCA, a
  catechism for young children, Keach) to get_confession.
  Source: https://github.com/reformed-standards/compendium
- Creeds.json, by NonlinearFruit. Public-domain texts. The compilation is under the
  Unlicense, and we take only documents it marks public domain: 21 confessions,
  catechisms, and rules of faith that the sources above lack. They power
  get_confession. Source: https://github.com/NonlinearFruit/Creeds.json
- Theology grounding corpus. Public-domain works from the Christian Classics Ethereal
  Library (CCEL, https://www.ccel.org), taken from "CCEL Paragraphs" by Jon Craton,
  CC BY-SA 4.0. We keep a curated set of public-domain theology as paragraphs with
  keyword and meaning search, for search_theology.
  Source: https://huggingface.co/datasets/jncraton/ccel-paragraphs
  CCEL's copyright policy: https://ccel.org/about/copyright.html

History data (bundled in db/social_sciences.db)
-----------------------------------------------
- Documenting the American South (DocSouth). University Library, The University of
  North Carolina at Chapel Hill, which made this material available. DocSouth Data
  packages: North American Slave Narratives, First-Person Narratives of the American
  South, and The Church in the Southern Black Community. CC BY 4.0. We use the plain
  texts, skip texts under 200 characters, and split each text into passages for search.
  Source: https://docsouth.unc.edu
- Wikisource. Public-domain texts: "The Cornerstone Speech" (Alexander H. Stephens),
  "A Model of Christian Charity" (John Winthrop), "The American Constitution and the
  Slave" (Frederick Douglass), and the Petition of Right (1628). Wikisource's
  transcriptions are shared under CC BY-SA 4.0. We keep the plain text.
  Source: https://en.wikisource.org
- Supreme Court Database coding (vote, issue area, chief justice, and each justice's
  vote). Cited as its makers ask: Harold J. Spaeth, Lee Epstein, Michael J. Nelson,
  Andrew D. Martin, et al. Supreme Court Database, Version 2026 Release 01 (terms from
  1946) and Legacy Release 07 (1791-1945). URL: https://scdb.psu.edu. Downloaded from
  https://scdb.la.psu.edu, which states no license. We join the coding to the case
  texts by SCDB case id.
- Presidential speeches. Cited as its makers ask: Miller Center of Public Affairs,
  University of Virginia. "Presidential Speeches: Downloadable Data."
  data.millercenter.org. The Miller Center states that the speeches are in the public
  domain and offers the data "for the use of the research and academic communities."
  We keep the speech transcripts only, not the Miller Center's introductions.
- Public domain and CC0, listed for completeness: Avalon Project documents (Yale Law
  School), National Archives founding and milestone documents, Supreme Court opinions (Caselaw Access
  Project, Harvard Law School), the Constitution Annotated (Congressional Research
  Service), the U.S. Constitution, the United States Code, the Illinois Constitution
  and Illinois Compiled Statutes, the U.S. Flag Code, Black's Law Dictionary (2nd ed., 1910), Webster's
  American Dictionary of the English Language (1828), a Wikidata slice of historical
  people, events, and places (CC0), and the congress-legislators data (CC0).

Reading Room (bundled in db/books.db)
-------------------------------------
- Reading Room theology books (CCEL). Public-domain works from the Christian Classics
  Ethereal Library, rebuilt into chapters from "CCEL Paragraphs" by Jon Craton,
  CC BY-SA 4.0, with chapter titles from CCEL's own tables of contents. We format the
  text for reading and drop front matter.
  Source: https://huggingface.co/datasets/jncraton/ccel-paragraphs
- Wikisource. Ann Radcliffe, "The Italian". Public domain. Wikisource's transcription
  is shared under CC BY-SA 4.0. Source: https://en.wikisource.org
- Public domain and CC0, listed for completeness: Project Gutenberg, Standard Ebooks
  (CC0 1.0), Internet Archive scans, and Project Gutenberg Australia (only titles
  public domain in the U.S.).

Optional live-proxied translations (NOT bundled, fetched at request time through the
publisher's API and never stored):
- ESV. English Standard Version, (c) Crossway, via api.esv.org. Requires an
  ESV_API_KEY (free, non-commercial only).
- NET. NET Bible(R), (c) Biblical Studies Press, via bible.org/labs (keyless,
  non-commercial). Quotations are marked "(NET)" and link to https://netbible.org.
